7.18.m:
I shall explain to you the means of awakening to that Brahman, / a secret to all yogins, an exposition of all yoga. / Then, O scorcher of foes, I shall declare to you the Māheśvara yoga. / The fire of brilliance, the fire born of desire, the fire of anger, the fire of the gaze, / and the digestive fire, the elemental self—these five are the fires of the body. / Prāṇa, apāna, samāna, udāna, and vyāna:
these five are the constant winds of the embodied being, originating within the body. / Originating from space, they dwell in the body, and the body rests upon them; / filled with flesh and blood, an accumulation of sinews, fat, and bones, / bound together by skin, foul-smelling, and covered with feces and urine;
transient like lightning or the arrival of clouds, resembling the falling of an earthen pot, / attended by every misfortune, and doomed to perish before long; / single in form, with nine gates, three pillars, and five deities, / filled with sense-objects and sense-organs, and afflicted by countless diseases.

