Quickly attaining the supreme yoga, he would be freed from bondage. / Having thus undertaken the vow, becoming a Pāśupata sage,
one should practice this supreme, secret knowledge of Maheśvara: / the known, the knowledge, and likewise the knower, as the triad exists in the body;
and what the self is, and how it is established in the three places, / and how the self indeed resides within the self in the subtle channels;
how it abides hidden everywhere in all beings, / like clarified butter in milk, O Vyāsa, knowing which one is liberated.
That self cannot in any way be known without knowledge. / Who knows that Maheśvara who is beyond form, sound, and taste? / Even so, O fortunate one, by the grace of Tryambaka, / I declare to you, O sinless one, the highest means to self-knowledge. / Indeed, in the beginning, this entire universe was darkness alone; / there were no gods, no wind, no sky, no sun, nor moon.
